\paragraph{Shape stationarity is prompt-type dependent.}
The shape stationarity component of differential depth response is observed primarily in continuation chains. Under elaboration and inspired-rewriting prompts, shape features (entropy\_std, tail\_decay) show near-zero depth correlation rather than the informative stationarity seen in continuation (Appendix~\ref{app:prompt_type}). One interpretation is that the structured prompt template itself stabilizes distributional shape regardless of generation depth, rendering shape features uninformative for depth discrimination in these settings. The drifting features (entropy\_mean, TTR, hapax\_ratio) remain consistent across all three prompt types, so the core depth signal---progressive lexical and entropic impoverishment---generalizes broadly. However, the full ``drifting central tendency + stationary shape'' pattern that we document for continuation chains should not be assumed to hold under arbitrary prompt types.

